I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and reusable analysis tools first, then build a phase inventory and establish a neutral-profile baseline before comparing structural candidates. I’ll keep the final artifacts tied to a reopened, replayed GSAS-II project and health-screen every retained CIF as requested.

The chemistry points first to Sr–W–O products with possible unreacted WO₃/SrCO₃, but I won’t assume a 1:1 product because no precursor ratio is reported. I’m now extracting peak families and querying the supplied crystallographic index for exact Sr/W/O records and plausible balance-motivated alternatives.

Initial peak-family evidence supports a scheelite SrWO₄ analogue: its low-, mid-, and high-angle families all track the data after an angular offset, while the extra 22–26° and 33–37° families require at least one additional phase. SrCO₃ accounts for part of the 25–37° set; I’m comparing all supplied WO₃ polymorph records and the exact Sr₃W₂O₉ record under the same positional scoring before any Rietveld scale refinement. The first WO₃ record checked has impossible 0.46 Å O–O contacts and is rejected as a source model.

All three working models now pass the required occupancy/contact health screen, including the adapted SrWO₄ model. The monoclinic WO₃ record COD 2311041 is the passing alternative to the contact-flagged record and reproduces the same diagnostic triplet near 23° with a common reflection-geometry shift. I’m moving into staged GSAS-II refinement with size ≥10 μm, zero strain, March ratio 1, zero roughness, fixed Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ metadata, and only the Bragg–Brentano specimen-height shift active as the angular correction.

The staged baseline is running. Scale, smooth background, and specimen-height shift have converged first; I’m letting the cell and common U/W/X profile replays finish, then I’ll reject any branch that drives a profile term negative/collapsed or a phase scale nonpositive.

The first simultaneous branch is invalid: with peaks initially offset by ~0.6°, the scale derivatives collapsed all three scales and drove specimen shift to an absurd value. I stopped it, discarded that state, and am restoring the pre-trial project. I’ll seed only the physically estimated ~1 mm specimen height from the observed peak offsets, establish positive scales with that correction fixed, and then release the same single shift after the peaks overlap.

The three-phase scales are positive and replay-stable, with the neutral profile already giving the expected independent families and provisional fractions near 61/22/17 wt%. The simultaneous U/W/X trial is not admissible because U refined negative. I’m restoring the saved W-only state exactly, then testing U fixed at its physical boundary (0) with W/X and later complementary phase broadening; the negative-U state will not be used for phase or fraction decisions.

The low/mid/high SrWO₄ audit is position-consistent (centers within about 0.02°) and the observed/calculated FWHM values agree well at 27°, 45°, and 73°. Remaining mismatch is mainly family intensity and some phase-dependent high-angle width, not unexplained peak positions. Because GOF remains ~2.43, I’m now doing matched-nuisance source tests: a second passing scheelite parent, the contact-flagged WO₃ record versus the passing record, and the leading triclinic WO₃ polymorph.

The matched source tests favor the passing CaWO₄-derived scheelite model for SrWO₄; the alternate WO₃ models were either chemically defective or less stable/worse fitting. I’m using that restored, replay-stable project as the common baseline and now testing phase broadening one phase at a time from neutral values.

The first broadening branches are already diagnostic: SrWO₄ crystallite size was singular at the neutral 10 µm value, while isotropic strain crossed to an unphysical negative value; WO₃ size also failed to move from neutral and did not converge. I’m discarding those branches and letting the remaining fresh-from-baseline trials complete.

WO₃ shows genuine hkl-dependent broadening: the [001] uniaxial strain model is positive, well determined, replay-stable, and lowers Rwp from 6.66% to 6.28%; the symmetry-distinct [010] challenge helps much less. I’m retaining [001] provisionally and now testing polarization and surface roughness against that exact profile before any texture term.

Polarization is the supported cross-phase intensity correction (0.753 ± 0.008). Adding roughness changes Rwp by only 0.004 percentage point, is highly correlated with polarization/scale, and drifts; the two-parameter roughness branch does not converge. I’m pruning roughness and challenging only simple March–Dollase models—axial and diagonal for each phase—against the restored polarization baseline.

All three simple texture terms remain positive, distinct from the tested diagonal alternatives, and replay-stable when combined; the current model is Rwp = 4.677% with fractions near 58.15/25.83/16.02 wt%. Before accepting those numbers, I’m auditing reflection families and repeating the leading source/watch-list tests under this exact nuisance model.

The restored source comparison changed the leading WO₃ assignment: the passing triclinic record gives a large, replay-stable improvement (Rwp 4.26% versus 4.66% for monoclinic), whereas the alternate SrWO₄ parent is worse. I’m checking whether that WO₃ gain reflects real peak splitting rather than extra cell freedom, then I’ll reconverge every nuisance term from the triclinic model before deciding.

The triclinic model has now replayed to max shift/esd = 0.016. Fixing polarization at 0.5 worsens Rwp sharply to 4.92% and shifts the fractions, so the angular intensity trend is real; I’m finishing the matched roughness substitution/addition tests to ensure polarization is not merely absorbing sample-surface effects.

For triclinic WO₃, removing its [001] March–Dollase term worsens Rwp to 4.85%; the [101] diagonal alternative converges reproducibly but remains much worse at 4.74%. This supports the retained [001] texture through distinct reflection families, not just covariance. The fresh size/strain branches are still running.

I’ve identified a three-phase model—scheelite SrWO₄, triclinic WO₃, and orthorhombic SrCO₃—and am finishing the required replay-stability, weak-phase, and final-project consistency checks before freezing the outputs. The current normalized fractions are about 58.3%, 26.1%, and 15.6%, respectively.

The source comparison is decisive under the same nuisance model: the ordered P‑1 WO₃ record gives Rwp 4.26% versus 4.66% for the passing P2₁/c alternative. I’m retaining P‑1, while treating the near-monoclinic metric as a structural-model uncertainty rather than inventing a WO₃ coexistence phase.

The clean one-phase-at-a-time profile test is behaving physically: removing WO₃ strain worsens Rwp from 4.26% to about 4.72%, with no singular values. I’m letting that simpler branch fully settle before reintroducing size and directional-strain alternatives from neutral values.

A better physically targeted branch emerged: WO₃ directional strain along [101] is replay-stable and lowers Rwp to 3.99% with the same parameter count, substantially better than [001] at 4.26%. I’m now challenging that result against the other triclinic axes and re-testing the complementary SrWO₄ broadening and texture terms before selecting it.

The [101] WO₃ strain model survives all six axial/diagonal challenges and remains the best (Rwp 3.993%); [010] is close at 3.995%, which I’ll report as profile-direction uncertainty. SrWO₄ [112] strain independently reappears from a neutral start, and the retained WO₃ [001] texture is strongly favored over both no-texture and diagonal [101] texture models.

The weak balance-motivated Sr₃W₂O₉ candidate fails the retention gate: starting scales differing by 10× both collapse to the numerical floor (10⁻¹²), with an effectively zero crystalline fraction. The restored three-phase model replays unchanged, so I’m freezing that model now.

Completed with a three-phase crystalline model:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| Scheelite SrWO₄ | 58.37% |
| Triclinic WO₃, P-1 | 26.06% |
| Strontianite SrCO₃ | 15.57% |

Final fit: Rwp 3.993%, Rp 2.965%, goodness-of-fit 1.398. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material; realistic systematic uncertainty is about ±1–2 wt%. All final CIFs passed health and geometry checks, and the project was reopened and replayed before export.

